WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.857
Engineering notes, IBM i high availability.

2
00:00:04.207 --> 00:00:11.226
PowerHA, or journal-based replication: two ways to keep an IBM i system running when the primary goes down.

3
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:16.064
Both answer the same question, at different layers.

4
00:00:16.414 --> 00:00:31.049
PowerHA works at the storage layer. With a SAN it uses Metro Mirror, Global Mirror or HyperSwap, and without one, Geographic Mirroring over internal disks. The protected workloads live in an independent auxiliary storage pool, an I-ASP.

5
00:00:31.399 --> 00:00:40.892
Journal-based replication works inside the operating system. Database changes are written to journals, and sent to the target with IBM i remote journaling.

6
00:00:41.242 --> 00:00:47.962
One copies disk blocks. The other copies logical changes. That decision explains almost everything that follows.

7
00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:55.912
First, the recovery point. Both can reach zero data loss for committed work, when they run synchronously.

8
00:00:56.262 --> 00:01:05.051
PowerHA holds each write until the target storage confirms it. Journal-based replication holds each commit until the remote journal acknowledges it.

9
00:01:05.401 --> 00:01:11.481
Run asynchronously, both stream changes ahead, and a sudden disaster loses only what was still in flight.

10
00:01:11.831 --> 00:01:15.671
The price of synchronous mode is one network round trip on every commit.

11
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.768
So how far is too far? There is no hard limit, only a rule of thumb.

12
00:01:22.118 --> 00:01:30.566
For busy transaction systems, architects aim for a round trip under about five to ten milliseconds. Lighter batch work can tolerate ten to twenty.

13
00:01:30.916 --> 00:01:37.401
Light in fibre adds about one millisecond of round trip for every hundred kilometres, before any switches or routers.

14
00:01:37.751 --> 00:01:42.231
Past that point, response times suffer, and replication moves to asynchronous.

15
00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:45.955
Bandwidth follows the layer.

16
00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:54.347
PowerHA sends every modified disk sector, whatever is inside it, which costs more during heavy random writes and the first full sync.

17
00:01:54.697 --> 00:02:08.244
Journal-based replication sends only the records that changed, and it can be tuned further: minimised entry data on the journal, Option forty two for HA journal performance, excluding objects that do not matter, and compression.

18
00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:12.991
Next, what the second system can do while it waits.

19
00:02:13.341 --> 00:02:22.066
With PowerHA, the replicated I-ASP is not usable while it is attached to the primary. Point-in-time access needs a FlashCopy snapshot.

20
00:02:22.416 --> 00:02:31.867
With journal-based replication, the target is running the whole time, so it can carry reporting, queries, role-swap tests and backups, while replication continues.

21
00:02:33.967 --> 00:02:35.353
Scope of protection.

22
00:02:35.703 --> 00:02:47.373
PowerHA protects what lives inside the I-ASP. User profiles, output queues and applications in the system pool need a cluster administrative domain, or a move into the I-ASP.

23
00:02:47.723 --> 00:03:02.592
Journal-based replication works across the system pool and every storage pool, with no restructuring. The database travels by remote journaling, and programs, IFS files and profiles are usually handled by the HA software built on top of it.

24
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.281
Corruption, and to be fair, it cuts both ways.

25
00:03:07.631 --> 00:03:18.042
Because PowerHA copies blocks, a physically damaged block is copied as it is. Journal-based replication moves only logical changes, so physical damage does not travel.

26
00:03:18.392 --> 00:03:26.221
But logical mistakes, like a bad batch update or a wrong delete, are replicated faithfully by both. Neither replaces a good backup.

27
00:03:26.571 --> 00:03:39.414
After an abnormal failover, PowerHA may need to rebuild indexes, which system-managed access path protection keeps in check. With journal-based replication, indexes are maintained as each change is applied.

28
00:03:41.533 --> 00:03:45.075
Neither approach is free. They simply charge at different times.

29
00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:55.558
PowerHA asks for its work up front: moving applications into I-ASPs, checking vendor support, and matching the storage. After that, it needs little daily attention.

30
00:03:55.908 --> 00:04:08.537
Journal-based replication avoids that rebuild, but asks for attention every day: watching queues and apply lag, authority drift and record locks, and regular audit-and-heal checks so the two copies never drift apart.

31
00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:13.077
Then there is distance, and regional risk.

32
00:04:13.427 --> 00:04:23.347
Synchronous PowerHA needs low-latency fibre, which usually keeps both sites in the same metro area, under the same storms, flood plains, fault lines and power grid.

33
00:04:23.697 --> 00:04:29.543
Asynchronous PowerHA can reach another region, but still needs matched storage and firmware at both ends.

34
00:04:29.893 --> 00:04:41.114
Journal-based replication runs over ordinary TCP/IP, across regions and power grids, and can feed a local HA node and a distant cloud recovery copy at the same time.

35
00:04:43.233 --> 00:04:44.407
So, which one?

36
00:04:44.757 --> 00:04:53.503
PowerHA fits organisations with standardised infrastructure, applications already in I-ASPs, and a wish for hands-off replication.

37
00:04:53.853 --> 00:05:02.728
Journal-based replication fits those that need hardware freedom, long distance, and a working target system, as long as the team owns the daily monitoring.

38
00:05:04.833 --> 00:05:07.372
Choose by your recovery goals, not by the brand.

39
00:05:07.722 --> 00:05:15.253
The full comparison, with references to IBM's documentation, is in the article at ikonstas70 dot github dot io.
